y error at bit i will cause z-bits from i+1 onward to be potentially wrong. But the specific pattern of wrong bits can help identify the swap.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. A carry error at bit i doesn't necessarily make ALL subsequent z-bits wrong. It depends on the specific values.\n\nFor example, if carry_i is wrong (1 instead of 0), then:\n- z_{i+1} = XOR(ps_{i+1}, wrong_carry_i) — might be wrong\n- But if the real carry is also 1 (due to generate/propagate from bit i+1), then z_{i+1} might be correct by coincidence.\n\nThis makes the simulation approach less reliable. We'd need multiple test inputs to distinguish between different error scenarios.\n\nHmm, let me think about a more robust approach.\n\n**Approach: Topological verification of the carry chain**\n\nThe carry chain in a correct adder has a specific topological structure. Each carry wire connects:\n- From the output of an OR gate (or AND gate for bit 0)\n- To the input of an XOR gate (for the sum) and an AND gate (for the propagate)\n\nI can verify this structure by checking the connectivity of each carry wire.\n\nFor each carry wire C (starting from carry_0):\n1. C is the output of a specific gate G1 (AND for bit 0, OR for other bits)\n2. C is an input to exactly two gates: the XOR gate for the sum and the AND gate for the propagate\n\nIf C is connected to the wrong gates, it's because of a swap.\n\nBut I need to identify which wires are carry wires, which brings us back to the same problem.\n\nOK, let me try a completely different approach. Let me think about this as a constraint satisfaction problem.\n\n**Approach: Verify each z-wire independently**\n\nFor each z-wire zNN:\n1. Find the gate that outputs zNN. Call it G.\n2. Check if G follows the expected pattern for a sum gate:\n   - G should be XOR\n   - One input should be the output of an XOR gate (partial sum)\n   - The other input should be the output of an OR gate (carry-in) — or AND(x00, y00) for z01\n3. If G doesn't f