re name \"abc\".\n\n  // Wait, I think my trace above should work! Let me re-examine.\n\n  // When I do findGateWithInputs(partialSumWire, carryWire, 'XOR'),\n  // I'm looking for a gate whose inputs are the actual wire names.\n  // The gate connectivity is based on actual wire names.\n  // So even if carryWire has a wrong name, the gate that uses it\n  // as input will still have it as in1 or in2.\n\n  // The only issue is: the OUTPUT wire names are wrong.\n  // So when I check if sumGate.out !== zi, that correctly identifies a swap.\n\n  // The trace should work end-to-end because:\n  // - Gate inputs are always correct (they reference actual wires)\n  // - Gate operations are correct\n  // - Only gate OUTPUTS are swapped\n\n  // So the trace I did above should be correct!\n  // Let me check if swapCandidates has duplicates or issues\n\n  const uniqueCandidates = [...new Set(swapCandidates)];\n  if (uniqueCandidates.length >= 8) {\n    // We might have found more than 8 due to cascading errors in the trace\n    // Let me verify by simulation: swap these wires and check if the circuit works\n\n    // Actually, let me re-examine: each swap pair is [zi, wrongWire]\n    // where zi is the expected output name and wrongWire is what the gate actually outputs\n    // This means zi and wrongWire are swapped.\n\n    // But some of these might be transitive: if z1 is swapped with a1,\n    // and a1 is swapped with z2, then z1 and a1 are directly swapped,\n    // and z2 and a1 are also involved... wait no, only 4 pairs, 8 wires total.\n\n    // Each swap pair means: the gate that should output zi actually outputs wrongWire,\n    // and the gate that should output wrongWire actually outputs zi.\n\n    // So if I have swapPairs, each pair [a, b] means wires a and b need to be exchanged.\n    // The total number of wires involved should be 8 (4 pairs).\n\n    // From swapPairs, collect all wires\n    const allWires2: string[] = [];\n    for (const [a, b] of swapPairs) {\n      